= Deductive reasoning — Simple =

'''Deductive reasoning''' is reasoning where the conclusion must follow if the premises are true.

Example:

<pre>
All mammals breathe.
Whales are mammals.
Therefore whales breathe.
</pre>

The important question is whether the conclusion follows from the premises.

A valid deduction can still start from a false premise.

If an argument is valid and its premises are true, it is called sound.

Deduction is different from [[Inductive reasoning/Simple|induction]], where the premises support a conclusion without guaranteeing it.

